' ============================================================
' HELLO WORLD - Your First DBasic Program
' ============================================================
' This file teaches the very basics of DBasic programming:
'   - How every program starts with SUB Main()
'   - How to print text to the screen with PRINT
'   - How to create a variable to store data
'   - How to ask the user for input with INPUT
'   - How to repeat something with a FOR loop
'
' Think of this as your "first day" of learning DBasic.
' Everything starts here!
' ============================================================

' Every DBasic program needs a SUB Main() -- this is where
' the computer starts running your code, like the front door
' of a house. Without it, the program would not know where
' to begin.
SUB Main()
    ' PRINT puts text on the screen for the user to see.
    ' The text inside the quotes is called a "string literal."
    ' Output: Hello, World!
    PRINT "Hello, World!"

    ' You can PRINT as many times as you want. Each PRINT
    ' starts a new line on the screen.
    ' Output: Welcome to DBasic
    PRINT "Welcome to DBasic"

    ' DIM creates a new variable -- think of it as a labeled
    ' box where you can store a value. Here we create a box
    ' called "name" that holds text (STRING).
    ' AS STRING means this variable can only hold text, not numbers.
    DIM name AS STRING

    ' This PRINT has a semicolon (;) at the end instead of
    ' ending normally. The semicolon tells DBasic: "don't go
    ' to the next line yet" -- so the user's typed answer
    ' will appear right after the question mark on the same line.
    ' Output: What is your name? (cursor stays on same line)
    PRINT "What is your name? ";

    ' INPUT pauses the program and waits for the user to type
    ' something and press Enter. Whatever they type gets stored
    ' in the variable "name" (our labeled box from above).
    INPUT name

    ' Now we PRINT a greeting using the value the user typed.
    ' The semicolons (;) glue pieces together on one line:
    '   "Hello, " + whatever they typed + "!"
    ' If the user typed "Alice", output would be: Hello, Alice!
    PRINT "Hello, "; name; "!"

    ' Demonstrate a loop
    ' DIM creates another variable, this time for a whole number
    ' (INTEGER). We will use "i" as a counter in the loop below.
    DIM i AS INTEGER

    ' FOR loops let you repeat code a specific number of times.
    ' This says: "Start i at 1, and keep going until i reaches 5,
    ' adding 1 each time." So the code inside runs 5 times,
    ' with i being 1, then 2, then 3, then 4, then 5.
    ' Output:
    '   Count: 1
    '   Count: 2
    '   Count: 3
    '   Count: 4
    '   Count: 5
    FOR i = 1 TO 5
        PRINT "Count: "; i
    NEXT
    ' NEXT marks the end of the FOR loop. When the program hits
    ' NEXT, it goes back to the FOR line, increases i by 1, and
    ' checks if i is still within the range (1 TO 5). If yes,
    ' it runs the code inside again. If no, it moves on.

END SUB
' END SUB marks the end of the Main subroutine.
' When the program reaches here, it is finished and exits.
